It's not a bad game, but it's too much confusing. I faced the 2 last chapters without a bullet (because i didn't have any ammo) and it worked. And that was exactaly when the game lost me.
PROS:
- Nice story (nothing too much, but cool enough)
- Cool tension moments. Specially fighting those gorilas
CONS:
- No objetive track. You get lost a lot;
- The game doesn't explain anything to you. Only in chapter 4 I realized that I could recharge my light (and nightvision). Only in chapter 5 I learned how the damn crossbow reload system worked;
- Grenades are useless. They take forever to explode, and creatures are too fast. You can try to slow them down by shooting, but you will fail a lot;
Maybe if you play the Spartan mode, that gives you more ammunition and read a walkthrough before playing. But playing blind as I did, I would not recommend. Let's hope the sequel is better.
5/10

Ys Origins is one of these great games you've never heard about and that has been lying on steam unknown, its worth unrecognized. Hier of a presitigious part gaming series that exists since the 80ies, Ys origins is a solid title If you don't mind anime art style and story tropes you'll have a solid isometric action RPG, with excellent bossfights and a demanding, nervous very satisfaying gameplay typical of japanesse games that has been embodied recently by darksouls.
/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
Ys Origins est ce genre de jeu qui traine sur steam, un peu perdu et oublié. Héritier d'une grande saga ayant commencé au cours des années 80, Ys origin est un titre généreux a condition de ne pas avoir d'alergie style manga que ce soit d'un point de vue esthétique ou narratif, par-ce que les clichés, vous allez en bouffer, chose qui n'empêche pas le jeu de conserver un certain charme et d'être une petite tuerie au niveau de son gameplay : boss-fights intressants et exigeants, très bon rythme de progression. Voilà du jeu japonais bien fichu et nerveux qui n'est pas sans rapeller Darksouls, même si la comparaison entre les deux s'arrête là.

Amazing audiovisual experience combined with fun puzzle gameplay. The audio part in particular deserves special mention. When you're not in the vehicle, you only hear certain parts of the level's BGM and it never ceased to amaze me how the different instruments in the songs were represented in the gameplay. Then you step into the vehicle and groove around the level to the complete song.
The shortcomings I can think of would be:
1) the somewhat repetitive puzzles, although I personally found there to be enough variety to make that a non-issue;
2) the dissonance between the movement/actions and the beat, as mentioned in another review;
3) the jellyfish are indeed infuriating. You'd think you can just slip into a line of jellyfish and move along with them, right? Nope, you're always either slower or faster and timing the boost to the 2nd beat doesn't necessarily move you at the same speed either;
4) and the last level, where they sort of ran out of steam at the end.
It also would've been nice if Harmony and/or Melody were playable or integrated into the gameplay somehow (you get a short glimpse of that in the finale). Having both as damsels in distress was a waste.
